
## 6. Concurrency

**What I used: no synchronization on `debug_mask` or `debug_level`.**

The argument that this is correct rests on four points.

**1. Neither read nor write can tear.** Both variables are single, naturally
aligned machine words — `unsigned long` and `int` on RV64. A load or store of an
aligned word is a single instruction and is indivisible with respect to other
harts. No reader can observe a partially updated value, so no lock is needed to
protect the integrity of either variable.

**2. Nothing in the kernel depends on the mask for correctness.** The mask is
advisory: it decides only whether a message is printed. No kernel invariant,
allocation, or lock-ordering rule reads it. A reader that races with a writer
sees either the old value or the new one, and both are acceptable — the only
consequence is that one debug line near the moment of the change is printed when
it might not have been, or vice versa. There is no state to corrupt.

**3. A lock would defeat the purpose.** The mask is read on nearly every
instrumented path, including `scheduler()`, which runs constantly on every hart.
Acquiring a spinlock on each `dprintf` evaluation would put an atomic
read-modify-write and an interrupt disable on those paths even when logging is
off — exactly the overhead the assignment's design goal forbids. The asymmetry
(read on every path, written by hand a few times per session) is the classic
case for leaving a single-word flag unsynchronized.

**4. Output interleaving is already handled.** `printk` takes the console lock,
which serializes the messages themselves.

**Two races I accept, and why.**

- `debug_mask` and `debug_level` are separate words, so `dbg only proc` followed
  by `dbg level trace` can be observed half-applied by a concurrent reader. The
  window is a few instructions and the effect is one mis-filtered log line.

- `dbg on` and `dbg off` are read-modify-write sequences performed in *user
  space* across two system calls (`GETMASK` then `SETMASK`), so two concurrent
  `dbg` invocations could lose an update. This is acceptable for an interactive
  tool used by one person at a shell prompt. If it mattered, the fix is a
  kernel-side `DBGCTL_ORMASK` / `DBGCTL_ANDMASK` pair performing the update
  atomically — the operation-code interface was chosen partly to leave room for
  exactly that.

**Locks held while logging.** Several `dprintf` calls execute with a spinlock
held: `allocproc()` returns with `p->lock` held, and both `kexit()` and
`scheduler()` print while holding `p->lock`. This is safe because `printk` is a
leaf — it acquires only the console lock, never sleeps, and never calls back
into instrumented code.

If a stricter guarantee were wanted, marking both variables `volatile` would
prevent the compiler from hoisting the load out of a loop. I did not do so,
because no instrumented loop re-reads the mask in a way that would make a hoist
observable.

